sections.s2_technical:
The two-argument form is the simplest but the most dangerous. It assumes every element produces a UNIQUE key. If two elements map to the same key, it throws IllegalStateException with the message "Duplicate key" — this is a runtime crash, not a compile error, making it a production bug waiting to happen.

```java
// (Recommended) WORKS when keys are unique:
Map<String, BigDecimal> tradeAmounts = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getTradeId, Trade::getAmount));
// {TRD-001: 245050, TRD-002: 176000, TRD-003: 330150}

//  CRASHES when keys duplicate:
// Two trades have symbol "RELIANCE" → duplicate key!
Map<String, BigDecimal> bySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount));
// IllegalStateException: Duplicate key RELIANCE!
```

The three-argument form adds a merge function that resolves key collisions. The merge function receives the existing value (already in the map) and the new value (from the current element) and returns the value to keep.

```java
// Keep FIRST value (existing wins):
Map<String, BigDecimal> firstBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        (existing, incoming) -> existing));
// RELIANCE → 245050 (first trade's amount kept, second discarded)

// Keep LAST value (incoming wins):
Map<String, BigDecimal> lastBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        (existing, incoming) -> incoming));
// RELIANCE → 123750 (second trade's amount replaces first)

// SUM values (aggregate):
Map<String, BigDecimal> totalBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        BigDecimal::add));
// RELIANCE → 368800 (245050 + 123750)

// MAX value:
Map<String, BigDecimal> maxBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        BigDecimal::max));
```

The four-argument form controls the Map implementation. HashMap is the default — it has no ordering guarantee. LinkedHashMap preserves insertion order. TreeMap sorts by key.

```java
// ORDERED: LinkedHashMap preserves the order elements were encountered
Map<String, BigDecimal> orderedBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        BigDecimal::add, LinkedHashMap::new));
// Iterates in the order symbols first appeared: RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC, INFY...

// SORTED KEYS: TreeMap
Map<String, BigDecimal> sortedBySymbol = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getSymbol, Trade::getAmount,
        BigDecimal::add, TreeMap::new));
// Iterates alphabetically: HDFC, INFY, RELIANCE, TCS...
```

A common pattern is collecting to a lookup Map — entity ID to entity:

```java
// Build a fast-lookup map: tradeId → Trade
Map<String, Trade> tradeLookup = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getTradeId, Function.identity()));
// Function.identity() = the element itself = t -> t

// With merge (if IDs could duplicate):
Map<String, Trade> safeLookup = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(Trade::getTradeId, Function.identity(),
        (existing, incoming) -> existing));
```

